SM(206,{"n":"Colossians 1.15-20","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Colossians 1.15-20.mp3","d":2987.2,"s":[[0.3,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancient faith dot com that's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen de Young teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Council of God."],[23.34,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[36.14,"So we will go ahead and get started when we get started in just a moment we're gonna be picking up in St."],[41.98,"Paul's epistle to the Colossians chapter one verse 15 we got about halfway through chapter one last time after the introduction give or take so I'm not gonna go back and redo the whole introduction because then we would only get through another half a chapter today."],[64.22,"But um sort of quickly catch us up."],[68.98,"Uh this epistle, St."],[70.38,"Paul is writing to uh Colossae, to an individual city, although that individual city is a small town, really, in the Lycus Valley near the larger cities of Laodice Laodicea and uh Heliopolis."],[89.54,"And so um it has a certain circular quality."],[95.14,"Even though it's written to a particular church."],[98.58,"Um, and we talked about how a sort of a lot of ink has been spilled uh because dissertations must be written and journal articles must be published on what the quote unquote Colossian heresy was."],[111.3,"We talked about how there's no real reason to assume that there's just sort of this one group or this one particular teaching or heresy that St."],[119.22,"Paul is addressing, um, especially since most of the attempts to reconstruct it have trouble trying to incorporate everything St."],[126.58,"Paul says, and that uh it's much more reasonable just to look at it as St."],[133.14,"Paul addressing various places where uh the people of the Christian community in Colossae might run into error, most of which seem to have to do with like some of the issues in Corinth coming to Christian teaching with sort of a pagan mindset and trying to sort of push it into the mold of their previous beliefs and therefore go ending up syncretizing, smooshing things together, right, worshiping the right God in the wrong way."],[165.86,"Kind of kind of issues."],[168.18,"And so St."],[169.38,"Paul is addressing is addressing those kinds of issues in general."],[173.38,"We don't need to go out of our way to try to reconstruct some whole system of belief, you know, that St."],[178.1,"Paul is addressing with every one of his comments."],[184.78,"In fact, that doesn't make a whole lot of sense."],[188.22,"So with that very, very brief uh recap of the introduction."],[194.94,"The first half of the chapter was mostly St."],[197.66,"Paul's greetings to them."],[201.98,"We saw as usual, St."],[203.74,"Paul lays out a lot of his themes for the epistle there in the opening."],[210.86,"But rather than running through all those again now, we'll be touching on those again and again, obviously, as we go through as we go through the text and he develops them."],[221.98,"So all that is to say, uh, let's get into the text."],[226.22,"It's a little quicker this time, since we're still at the beginning of the book."],[229.74,"Unless anybody has any questions or comments or allegations to hurl or anything else."],[238.3,"Tweets they want to read aloud."],[240.78,"Okay. We'll go ahead and get started then in uh St."],[245.02,"Paul's Epistle to the Colossians, uh, chapter one, verse 15."],[250.3,"St. Paul has just talked about Christ having delivered us from uh the powers of darkness, brought us into his kingdom, and redemption through his blood."],[262.3,"And so now, as we pick up in verse 15, he's going to talk more about Christ and who Christ is."],[271.98,"So, verse 15."],[273.34,"He is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn over all creation."],[279.5,"We'll go ahead and stop there."],[281.58,"Because this verse in the history of the church has caused no end of consternation."],[281.9,"Because this was, as you might imagine, one of the Arian's favorite verses."],[296.66,"This is actually really well translated here."],[300.82,"That they translated it over."],[304.02,"That Christ is the firstborn over all creation."],[309.94,"Right? As opposed to the firstborn of all creation."],[316.66,"But we'll talk about the first part of the verse first."],[319.7,"That he is the image of the invisible God."],[324.18,"So we'll start there with that part."],[326.82,"Right. So when he talks about the invisible God, who is he talking about?"],[332.5,"The Father. Right?"],[334.02,"The Father. So he's identifying the person of the Father as the invisible God, and Christ is the image."],[341.78,"Right? So why is why is this important?"],[344.1,"St. Paul isn't just coming up with some new theology."],[348.42,"St. Paul isn't sitting there thinking, well, okay, I believe that Jesus is divine, uh, but I'm a monotheist, so how does that work?"],[358.74,"Right? How can I make this fit together."],[362.34,"St. Paul is drawing on what pretty much every Jewish person alive in the first century already believed."],[371.46,"Right? We've talked about this before in other epistles."],[374.74,"But it was commonly and well known in Judaism in the first century AD and in preceding centuries, that there was more than one person who was God."],[387.06,"There's more than one person who is Yahweh."],[393.18,"And this is referred to in different ways in Jewish literature."],[400.14,"Sometimes it's referred to as uh there being two powers in heaven, or that there's a second power in heaven."],[408.14,"And the word that's being translated power there is the Greek word exusia, um, which really means freedom to act."],[420.7,"Right? That's that's not the word where we talk about the heavenly powers."],[426.3,"That's usually uh dinameon."],[429.34,"It's uh power like denomis."],[433.82,"They're the power that they have ability, whereas exusia is more the sense of freedom."],[440.06,"So the idea was right."],[442.3,"Obviously, Yahweh, the God of Israel is free."],[447.34,"The angelic powers act at his behest."],[451.9,"So they aren't powers, they don't have authority in that sense, they don't have their own authority, they operate under his authority."],[459.58,"So the idea of there being a second power in heaven, right, is the idea that there is another person who also has that freedom."],[473.66,"And therefore has a different relationship to Yahweh than angelic beings do."],[480.9,"Right. Um. How did that work in Judaism?"],[487.14,"Well, they they they're there are other terminology."],[490.18,"They're sometimes they refer to the greater Yahweh and the lesser Yahweh."],[494.18,"Um that second figure, that second person, second hypostasis is the language."],[500.82,"That language predates Trinitarian stuff."],[505.38,"Um second hypostasis, the second person is identified with this figure who appears all through the Old Testament, and that appears is important."],[515.7,"Right. So you've got this figure of the angel of the Lord."],[522.42,"Who appears all through the Old Testament."],[524.26,"The word of the Lord is described in these personal terms."],[528.58,"The word of the Lord appears to people."],[530.74,"The word of the Lord stands next to Samuel's bed."],[537.06,"And calls out to him."],[538.42,"The word of the Lord comes and pokes Jeremiah."],[544.42,"Um in personal terms."],[546.82,"You've got um places where people see Yahweh, the God of Israel."],[555.78,"Right? So the elders go with Moses partway up Mount Sinai, and it describes this person who they see."],[565.94,"Right? Who's Yahweh, the God of Israel."],[570.18,"They see him and they say eat and drink."],[573.14,"And you've got Yahweh shows up with two angels and eats with Abraham and Sarah."],[577.94,"Right? So there's all these places where people see God."],[581.06,"And then there's also all these other places in the Old Testament where it says you can't see God."],[587.86,"Right? No one can see God and live."],[590.5,"The most famous one being in the book of Exodus, where the same chapter it says that Moses on Mount Sinai talked to God face to face, the way a man speaks to his friend."],[600.46,"And then a few verses later in the same chapter, he asks God to show him his glory, and God says, You can't see me my face and live."],[613.5,"Right? And so Jewish people read this."],[620.14,"And so they said there's gotta be this second person."],[623.18,"So there's there's an invisible God who you can't see."],[628.78,"Who no one can see and live, and no one ever sees, and then there's Yahweh who you can't see, and then there's also Yahweh who appears to people and talks to people, and right, and but this is the this is Yahweh."],[645.34,"That's there's just the one God."],[648.38,"Yahweh, the God of Israel, but there's these two different persons."],[653.18,"And this shows up all over the place in the New Testament that this is their understanding of who Jesus is."],[658.06,"The most famous one is in John chapter 1."],[661.66,"When he when St."],[662.7,"John says no one has seen God at any time."],[665.9,"And and St."],[666.62,"John isn't going back and like retconning the Old Testament."],[670.54,"Saying all those things never happened."],[672.3,"He says, No one has seen God at any time, but the unique God who is in the bosom of the Father has made him known."],[680.46,"So that's who you've been seeing."],[683.82,"Is there any like is there any ancient uh Hebrew literature that might this um dispute between like modalism versus the two divine figures that they might have already?"],[694.78,"Right. Well, that comes later."],[697.18,"I feel like that was in like first century, but well, no, that comes even later than that."],[700.7,"Okay. Yeah, let me, I'll get there, yeah, I'll get there in a second so the uh the other big figure is of course the son of man in Daniel right you have the ancient days of the Son of Man who's the Son of Man same figure right and that's why when Christ identifies himself as the Son of Man to the high priest the high priest says that's blasphemy right identifying yourself as the Messiah was not blasphemy you might be wrong right but the high priest reacts as if he said he's God but he identifies himself as the Son of Man so uh you you also have of course this verse we just read from St."],[739.86,"Paul right you've got the invisible God and then you've got the image of the invisible God who appears to people and makes him known right so St."],[749.14,"Paul's picking up on that same tradition so that that the consistent testimony of the New Testament is that second person became incarnate as Jesus Christ right we saw in in Philippians chapter 2."],[763.54,"Remember the whole hymn about Christ's incarnation right so this then causes a crisis in rabbinic Judaism following on after Christianity right because there's all this literature first Enoch second that all talk about this second figure all of this literature and so rabbinic Judaism sees Christianity making use of all this tradition and literature and repudiates the whole thing."],[793.78,"So in the second century, the rabbis, after the destruction of the temple, after the end of the Barkokva rebellion, who are reconstructing Judaism, uh repudiate all of those texts and declare this idea that there are two powers in heaven to be a heresy."],[812.22,"Now, this causes them a problem, though, and this problem shows up in the Talmud."],[817.58,"Uh, because there were earlier rabbis who are very famous rabbis, like Rabbi Akiva, who was around in the Barkokra Rebellion, who were known to believe this."],[829.26,"Because everybody did."],[830.86,"Who are known to believe this."],[832.22,"They want to keep Rabbi Akiva."],[834.7,"They want to get rid of this idea."],[837.5,"And so they have this problem."],[839.74,"And so there are these stories in the Talmud that try to deal with this, and they deal with it in different conflicting ways."],[848.94,"Right? So, the what about Rabbi Akiva is they basically say, well, yeah, Rabbi Akiva believed that."],[855.42,"Uh, but uh he got corrected."],[860.78,"He got corrected by an angel and told that no."],[863.82,"Um the phrase that's in the Talmud is there's there's one for judgment and one for mercy."],[870.78,"And what that's referring to is that's referring to the thrones in uh in Daniel with the Son of Man."],[880.62,"Right. So they say, well, this is basically modalism."],[883.98,"They say there's only one person."],[886.62,"One God Yahweh, but he appears in different ways."],[890.86,"He appears as an old man to show mercy, and he appears as a young man to bring judgment."],[899.34,"And justice. And so those two thrones aren't for two different persons."],[903.5,"Those two thrones are the thrones he uses for these two different purposes."],[910.5,"Right. So that's one solution."],[913.62,"However, later in the Talmud, in uh uh Tractate Hagiga, uh, there's another story about a rabbi who believed in the two powers of heaven, who uh this one they weren't trying to salvage, um, where uh he has a mystical vision where he ascends into heaven, and when he's there, he sees someone sitting."],[938.82,"And someone who's sitting, that was the mark of authority in the ancient world."],[944.9,"So, like in liturgy now, uh, because we've got we've got pews, it's the opposite of what it would have been in the ancient world."],[952.58,"In the ancient world, when I gave a sermon, I would sit and you guys would all stand."],[960.9,"As a mark of teaching authority."],[963.22,"So everybody else sitting down and me standing up and speaking."],[966.74,"It was the exact opposite."],[968.02,"So he sees this other figure in heaven sitting down besides God."],[974.9,"Who we call God the Father, and so he believes that there are two powers in heaven."],[981.14,"But then it's explained to him that oh no, that was an archangel who was being naughty by sitting down, and so that guy gets like thrown out of heaven and cursed for having dared to think that there were two powers in heaven, and the archangel gets whipped by the other angels because he was naughty and uh sat down in front of in front of a human and confused him."],[1009.7,"So, yeah, I know it's a weird story."],[1011.62,"Uh so but the idea is, so that's another way of dealing with it of oh no, there was just there's there's like a really high-ranking archangel."],[1021.66,"Right, yeah. Yeah."],[1023.26,"All the Metatron stuff in 3rd Enoch and all of that comes later in the rabbinic period as a way to try to salvage that literature."],[1031.5,"And salvage that tradition without going down the Christian route of saying there's more than one person of the Godhead."],[1039.74,"Um so that's what St."],[1041.42,"Paul's referring to here with him with Christ being the image of the invisible God."],[1046.14,"Right? Because yet at first glance in English, that that doesn't make any sense."],[1050.54,"If something's invisible, how can you have an image of it?"],[1054.94,"Right? I took a picture of something invisible."],[1058.38,"Well, okay, right."],[1061.34,"Um the idea is that he is the person."],[1067.66,"The person of the Godhead who reveals the Father."],[1072.14,"You don't see the Father, you see the Son."],[1074.46,"You see Christ, and by knowing Christ, you know who the Father is."],[1078.94,"See all those long passages I read while everybody's getting worried about how long the service is going to be on Holy Thursday."],[1089.66,"During that first gospel reading in St."],[1092.06,"John's Gospel. Where he says, you know, they ask Christ, show show us the Father."],[1099.18,"He says, Have you been with me?"],[1100.46,"So if you've seen me, you have seen the Father."],[1103.58,"Right? Not saying they're the same person, but he's the image of the Father."],[1109.02,"And that image language is is uh what is picked up by all the fathers of the First Ecumenical council and Second Ecumenical council."],[1119.9,"The Gregory, St."],[1121.58,"Basil, all use this image language, and they're getting it from St."],[1124.74,"Paul here, in terms of the relationship, right, between father and son, but he's the image who reveals God to us, not just in the incarnation, comes to its full its fullness, its fulfillment in the incarnation, but it begins all the way back."],[1140.9,"You know, somebody was walking in the garden."],[1145.14,"Right in Genesis 3, which means they had feet and legs."],[1150.82,"Yeah. Okay, so that's first half."],[1156.74,"So now, second half, firstborn over all creation."],[1160.74,"And this is why that's a good translation."],[1163.06,"Uh the Arians tried to make this be the firstborn of all creation."],[1167.62,"So they're trying to use firstborn, which is prototokos, uh, related to Theotokos."],[1174.02,"The one who gave birth to God, Prototokos is the one who was born first."],[1178.82,"So literally firstborn, uh, and tried to take it in that temporal sense."],[1184.74,"Firstborn of all creation, meaning he's the first thing God created, is how they wanted to interpret it."],[1193.46,"But firstborn over all creation is correct because firstborn in the ancient world, particularly the ancient Jewish world, was not primarily a chronological issue."],[1208.9,"It was an issue of status."],[1213.38,"The firstborn was the heir."],[1216.46,"Right. So when someone died, the person who is designated as their firstborn inherited everything, 100%, and then they distributed it to everyone else."],[1228.1,"But they received everything."],[1231.74,"Right? And so that meant this is the next head of the household."],[1241.58,"In the Roman terms, this is the next Potter Familius."],[1244.54,"This is the next."],[1251.82,"And you see this pop up a few places, like in the Gospels, there's the place where the man shows up and asks Jesus to tell his brother to divide the inheritance with him."],[1262.06,"Meaning his brother was the firstborn, inherited everything, and just decided to keep it."],[1266.38,"And didn't give any to his brother."],[1268.38,"So that's not fair."],[1269.82,"Jesus, you go tell him to give me some of the inheritance."],[1273.9,"And Jesus basically tells him, Why are you worried about this stuff?"],[1277.18,"Come, you know, follow me, be a disciple."],[1279.74,"Let him have, you know, let him have the stuff, you know."],[1283.5,"Um so it shows up there."],[1285.5,"This is it."],[1286.06,"We talked about this in Romans 9."],[1287.58,"This is really integral to the argument St."],[1289.5,"Paul's making in Romans 9, but he's using it in Romans 9 in terms of Jacob and Esau, remember, where Esau came out a few seconds earlier, even though they were twins, but it's Jacob who has that firstborn status."],[1303.18,"Ishmael was born first, but Isaac has the firstborn status."],[1308.38,"So the reason overall creation is the correct translation is that's what St."],[1313.9,"Paul is saying."],[1316.06,"That Christ has this status over all of creation, not just all of humanity, right, but over everything."],[1324.62,"His inheritance is everything."],[1327.34,"Matthew 28, all authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."],[1334.9,"Right? The word for authority there is exusia, by the way, since we already brought it up."],[1340.26,"Um all authority, all power."],[1343.46,"Dominion had been given to Christ, and then Christ shares it with us."],[1348.5,"That's that co-heirs, fellow heirs with Christ language that St."],[1352.1,"Paul's always using when he talks about us becoming sons of God."],[1355.86,"Right. So that's how it that's right."],[1359.06,"That's how it's connected."],[1360.42,"That's why he shares in his rule, he shares in his priesthood, he shares in these things with us."],[1370.42,"So those are the two things that St."],[1372.82,"Paul is saying in this verse, both of which are big things."],[1375.78,"Number one, he's the visible or bodily Yahweh of the Old Testament."],[1382.9,"He's he's the God who reveals the invisible God."],[1386.98,"His father. And number one, and then uh number two, that he also has this authority as the firstborn over all creation."],[1397.38,"Right? And both of those are connected to each other, and that it's identifying Christ as this son of man figure who inherits all the nations."],[1406.18,"And this figure of the Old Testament."],[1408.58,"Yes, sir. Well, there was already the ancient understanding of the firstborn being primary uh subject of authority rather than like status of like time of birth."],[1417.3,"Yeah. Why why we translated it from both the over?"],[1421.7,"Well, it wasn't I mean that's English."],[1424.82,"Oh. It was how they wanted to interpret it."],[1429.46,"That was how the Arians were trying to were putting this forward as a verse that supported their position."],[1435.38,"And they were misunderstanding or deliberately, you know, twisting the verse words."],[1442.22,"He firstborn, aha, see."],[1447.58,"Yeah. Yeah, but he's he's for a Jewish audience, they knew what he was talking about."],[1455.58,"But this is colossally Gentiles."],[1458.06,"Right, right. There's only a small Jewish community."],[1460.94,"But in terms of the Old Testament and Old Testament theology, from their perspective, he's just saying Jesus is that person."],[1470.06,"Right? Jesus is that person who you know of from the Old Testament, these these documents and all these things."],[1478.94,"So, yeah, for us, we have to unpack it a lot more because uh a lot of that theology was buried for a long time."],[1486.78,"Right? We talked before, I won't go into it again, but we've talked before about sort of the chronic problem in Western theology of sort of taking the local Jewish people at the local synagogue and assuming that's what Judaism has always been."],[1502.94,"Uh projecting that all the way back into ancient Israel sometimes, you know, as if Israelites in a thousand BC like went to a synagogue on Saturday and heard a sermon and read their Torahs, you know, at home."],[1517.42,"Right, which, you know, no."],[1524.38,"Verse 16. For by him all things were created that are in heaven and that are on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers, all things were created through him and for him."],[1540.14,"Right? So that for is a because."],[1543.62,"So if you had any doubts about the Aryan interpretation, this kind of messes it up."],[1550.18,"Right? He has that firstborn status over all creation because he created everything."],[1559.46,"Right? He created everything."],[1563.06,"Everything in heaven, everything on earth, everything visible, everything invisible."],[1568.18,"And then just to really bore down on it, thrones, dominions, principalities, powers."],[1574.42,"So all those other spirits, all those pagan gods you used to worship, all the angels, all the demons, all the everything, Christ created those."],[1585.14,"Right? Which means he ain't one of them."],[1589.86,"Right? He isn't one of them."],[1591.46,"So they're created by him, and then they were created through him, and they were created for him."],[1598.42,"So St. Paul does a lot of cool things with prepositions."],[1602.82,"Right. So by him means what?"],[1607.7,"Meaning it's not right."],[1609.14,"Meaning it's not just God the Father did it, and he was there watching."],[1616.9,"Right? Which means again, he can't be the first thing created because everything that was created was created by him."],[1627.7,"So he's involved in it through him."],[1631.3,"Means it's he and the father cooperating."],[1633.38,"It's not him instead of the Father."],[1635.86,"Why would that be important?"],[1639.7,"It's not the Father and not Him, but it's not Him and not the Father."],[1643.38,"If you think about, for example, Platonism."],[1646.18,"Oh, emanation. Right, you've got a you've got a Most High God who's sort of detached from everything and invisible and unknowable."],[1647.94,"And you've got a demiurge down below who creates everything."],[1658.46,"So St. Paul St."],[1659.58,"Paul's clarifying that's not what he's saying."],[1662.14,"Right? They're made by him."],[1664.3,"They're also made through him."],[1666.22,"So he and the Father are creating everything together is the idea."],[1671.58,"Right? They're both actively involved."],[1673.5,"They're both working at the same time."],[1675.9,"This is this is where the later fathers are going to get the idea that the Holy Trinity has one energy."],[1682.86,"Right? They don't work separately."],[1686.14,"Um, let alone against each other."],[1689.02,"But that they're united in everything they do, including creation."],[1693.42,"And for him, right, is the that eschatological for him to have this firstborn status, this inheritance status."],[1703.98,"So that at Christ's ascension and his enthronement, right, as it's described in the book of Daniel with the Son of Man, as it's described in the gospel at the end of the Gospels at the beginning of Acts, right, that Christ then receives all creation."],[1718.54,"So creation is the inheritance of the firstborn."],[1722.3,"Right. Right, meaning everything."],[1725.26,"Except the Father."],[1730.78,"Is he ever described as uncreated?"],[1734.3,"Christ. Well. I mean it's implied here."],[1746.98,"Because it just said everything created is created by him and through him and for him."],[1751.74,"Which means he can't be created."],[1753.1,"Right. And he can't be one of the things created if all things that were created unless he created himself."],[1763.62,"Which he didn't do."],[1768.26,"Yeah, I'm trying to think of the New Testament, if it's used."],[1776.9,"I mean, it's used later, of course, to explain what's going on, like here."],[1781.94,"Um. I'll look into it and get back to you if I can find a."],[1790.5,"Yes, sir. We probably just as humans have a hard time distinguishing between creation and procession, because obviously we've never seen something proceeded."],[1799.78,"Or be begotten."],[1800.74,"Yeah. Yeah, begotten."],[1802.02,"Yeah. It's a very foreign thing for us to even imagine what being of the Father is."],[1807.7,"So I can find it."],[1809.06,"But say St."],[1809.78,"Paul here clearly has this category, things created, and Jesus is outside of it."],[1815.86,"Yeah. Right. I mean, that's clearly what he's saying."],[1819.3,"Philip gave an explanation of the begotten and the proceeding and the uh in the Nicene Creed."],[1826.58,"He said, all we can tell is that these are two different things."],[1829.7,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah, we don't know what it means."],[1832.42,"But they're they're not the same."],[1833.86,"Right. We repeat back what we were told."],[1837.62,"And we don't try to go beyond that."],[1840.74,"Because uh. Okay."],[1847.78,"So verse 17."],[1850.5,"He is before all things, and in him all things consist."],[1856.18,"Okay. So, before all the created things, he already exists."],[1857.62,"Notice he says he is before all things, not he was before all things."],[1867.5,"Right? So again, it's not that he comes into being before all the other stuff does."],[1872.94,"Right? All the stuff comes into being, he already is."],[1879.26,"And then in him all things consist."],[1884.54,"Yeah, it's the idea there in the Greek is literally that in him all things hold together."],[1891.18,"Um that is most likely a reference to the way creation is talked about in the Old Testament."],[1900.38,"Uh is not as this one-time event."],[1904.14,"We tend to think of creation in a very deistic way."],[1908.38,"Where God sort of creates everything and then it kind of rolls on."],[1912.94,"Like he winds up the clock and then it goes."],[1915.82,"You used to have to wind clocks, guys."],[1918.78,"Uh anyway. Um, get off my law."],[1925.5,"Anyway. Uh he sort of winds it up and it goes."],[1931.18,"And it goes."],[1932.54,"Um, but that's not how creation is talked about in the Old Testament."],[1935.82,"Creation is an ongoing perpetual thing."],[1939.66,"Right? So that, you know, every child that's conceived is created by God in the womb."],[1947.34,"Right? And the crops and the plants that grow are being created by God."],[1952.22,"Right? That God is the one doing this in this ongoing way."],[1955.98,"He's actively involved, right, all the way through."],[1959.66,"And this is part of the building, this is one of the building blocks of the concept of the divine energies, God working in creation."],[1963.18,"This is what is this way of talking in the Old Testament."],[1965.46,"Right? And so St."],[1971.38,"Paul is here saying that Christ is not only involved back there in ye olden times, however long ago it was."],[1980.5,"That everything was set in motion."],[1982.5,"He wasn't just there helping wind the clock, but he's been part of it all the way through."],[1989.86,"Right? The ongoing sustaining of creation and the ongoing unfolding of God's creative activity."],[2000.9,"You know, like seeking the truth from God."],[2002.98,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah, that's one of the reasons we that's sometimes referred to as the creation psalm."],[2012.82,"The rest were so."],[2043.86,"Right? This isn't just an analogy, but a body is sort of your nexus of powers and potentialities."],[2053.14,"It's how you act and express yourself in the world."],[2057.54,"Right? So when it says he's the head of the body, the church."],[2060.74,"It's not just a question of authority."],[2063.3,"But as the head, he's the one who directs it."],[2067.06,"And then his body is the means by which, meaning the church is the means by which Christ communicates with the world."],[2076.06,"He interacts with the world, he goes out into the world."],[2079.66,"Hands, feet, all the different parts."],[2082.46,"That's all done through the church."],[2085.58,"Right? So he has the authority in the church, and he acts through the church."],[2090.54,"Within creation. So this shows us St."],[2095.9,"Paul's view of how the church relates to the creation."],[2100.54,"Right? Christ is the creator and sustainer of the cosmos."],[2106.78,"And so uh the church in the midst is the means through which he is doing it."],[2118.06,"And he's the beginning."],[2120.38,"He's the firstborn from the dead."],[2122.86,"So then in all things he has the preeminence."],[2126.46,"So he's now come full circle back to what he means by firstborn, the preeminence, right, in all things."],[2137.66,"Verse 19, for it pleased the Father that in him all the fullness should dwell, and by him to reconcile all things to himself, by him, whether things on earth or things in heaven, having made peace through the blood of his cross."],[2157.82,"Okay, so pleased."],[2162.78,"We have to remember, we get this through the King James."],[2167.66,"Pleased and pleasure in Elizabethan English are words referring to will or desire."],[2172.94,"It doesn't mean pleased like made happy."],[2175.62,"Right? Example of one of our traparias of the resurrection."],[2181.78,"We say that Christ took pleasure in ascending the cross."],[2185.14,"Right? We don't mean like, boy, that was a fun time."],[2190.58,"Crucifixion, not a fun time."],[2193.22,"Not a pleasurable experience."],[2195.06,"That's took pleasure there is this sense that he did it willingly."],[2199.86,"Christ did it voluntarily."],[2201.54,"He did it willingly."],[2202.74,"He ascended the cross."],[2204.18,"He wasn't nailed to a cross and pushed up in the air, but he willingly did it."],[2209.38,"So this is the same idea."],[2211.46,"This is this is it's it's the Father's will that he is going to reconcile the whole creation to himself through Christ."],[2225.14,"So notice something here that we now have this second category."],[2245.38,"Right? There's no mention, there's no sin, there's no need for reconciliation anywhere in there."],[2252.02,"This is all true."],[2254.18,"And then we get this second element here that St."],[2257.06,"Paul brings in, which is apart from this, sin comes into the world."],[2264.1,"Right? And so the Father wills that also through Christ, right, he's going to take care of the sin issue."],[2275.54,"Right? Because all of creation, this whole creation that was created by, through, and for Christ is now affected by the power of sin."],[2283.82,"Which means it's alienated from God."],[2287.18,"And so additionally, right, in addition to what was already going to happen with the incarnation and ascension of Christ and Him coming to His inheritance and authority, he is also going to reconcile the creation to God through His blood."],[2309.02,"Now notice St."],[2312.3,"Paul says he's reconciling all things to himself, and then to clarify it by him, whether things on earth or things in heaven."],[2319.58,"So he's talking about all creation."],[2321.42,"He's not talking about humans."],[2324.94,"Right? All creation includes all the humans."],[2328.62,"But it also includes rocks, trees, fish, fowl."],[2334.78,"Everything else. Right?"],[2336.14,"The whole creation that's been that's been tainted by sin."],[2340.3,"So what is happening with the blood of Christ's cross in making peace, right, is happening to the whole creation."],[2351.5,"It's not something that's just happening to humans."],[2354.94,"Because the problem isn't just humans."],[2358.7,"Right? The problem is sin has tainted now the whole creation."],[2363.18,"And so create that whole creation that's been tainted by sin has to be cleansed and purified."],[2369.98,"So that it can come back into the presence of God."],[2373.02,"So if you have something sinful and you bring it into the presence of God, what happens?"],[2377.82,"It's consumed by his holiness."],[2379.18,"It's consumed by his holiness."],[2380.86,"Right. How do we see the impact of sin on a non-human creation?"],[2389.7,"I might be guessing."],[2391.78,"Yeah. Well, I don't recommend going and trying to play with an alligator."],[2397.22,"Right. Right? There's now enmity between human beings and animals."],[2402.66,"And human beings and each other."],[2405.62,"And Adam in the ground, so it brings forth thorns and thistles."],[2410.74,"Right? Natural disasters which we I mean we see a hurricane or an earthquake as a disaster."],[2418.66,"Yeah. But how do are they because of the enmity between us?"],[2424.42,"Yes. Yeah. Yeah."],[2428.98,"The the uh regardless of how one politically feels about climate change."],[2437.3,"The idea that human activity and sinful human activity and exploitation can cause negative effects on the world and the environment is basic to Christianity."],[2453.86,"Right? Without going into all the particulars of climate change."],[2457.38,"But, you know, think what you want."],[2460.02,"But that's but that general I concept is pretty basic to Christianity."],[2467.78,"Right? And, you know, yeah, you go and pollute your river and then try and drink out of it is a pretty materialist like object lesson."],[2476.5,"Um but but uh yeah, it affects everything."],[2481.62,"It affects everything."],[2483.14,"It affects people physically."],[2485.78,"Right? It's what leads to physical corruption and death."],[2491.66,"Right? Right. It's not mentioned."],[2505.09,"Okay. We don't know for sure."],[2509.01,"There's also the whole inside the garden, outside the garden thing."],[2511.73,"Yeah, is that there's a whole bunch of complications to that."],[2514.77,"But the idea of enmity."],[2517.49,"So this is why we see like in the lives of saints, certain saints can go and be and be with wild animals, and there's no Saint Seraph and the sorrow."],[2527.01,"Saint Seraphim with the bear."],[2528.37,"Yeah, there are lots of I mean."],[2532.61,"I mean, say St."],[2533.57,"Mark talks about that in a verse nobody notices."],[2537.57,"Uh when he describes Christ's temptation in the wilderness, it says after his temptation he remained 40 days in the wilderness among the wild animals."],[2547.73,"Right? In the out in the uh in the wilderness, right."],[2553.57,"So all of all of that, it it affects all of creation, all creation has to be purified."],[2558.45,"And we see this, I mean, most especially in the Day of Atonement ritual in the Old Testament."],[2565.89,"Right? Where the blood is taken."],[2569.33,"It's not like blood to feed the blood god."],[2572.85,"It's it's the blood is taken and used to purify the physical objects."],[2580.53,"The physical accoutrement inside the tabernacle and rededicate them."],[2587.97,"Right? And at the ratification of the covenant at Mount Sinai, blood is taken and sprinkled on the people."],[2594.21,"We see somebody spattered with blood and we don't think it, you know, cleansed and purified."],[2599.57,"We think the opposite."],[2600.53,"How are you gonna get that blood stain out of your rope?"],[2602.93,"Right? But that it's cleansing and purification."],[2605.89,"That's what blood's used for."],[2609.25,"Right? Because sin, and the idea is sin is the taint and the power of death."],[2617.25,"Right? And blood is life."],[2620.77,"Right? So you smear life on it."],[2623.09,"Right to overcome death."],[2626.81,"So that same language is the language that's applied to Christ's blood."],[2631.69,"Again, if you read carefully in the New Testament, in Hebrews, in 1 John, right, Christ's blood purifies us, cleanses us from sin."],[2643.85,"Right? And so St."],[2645.53,"Paul is talking about this not just with humans, but at the cosmic level."],[2649.77,"Right? Where he's treating the whole cosmos."],[2653.85,"Right? So this is St."],[2655.37,"Paul's really going all the way back to Genesis 1 here."],[2660.25,"Genesis 1 is deliberately structured in the way that in the ancient Eries they structured narratives of temple building."],[2672.25,"Temple building. So this is the idea that Eden is constructed as this temple."],[2678.25,"Adam is created and put there to serve in that temple."],[2682.57,"Right? As a priest."],[2713.41,"Being the priest that serves in that temple."],[2718.77,"And for that destiny to come to fruition."],[2723.01,"Christ's blood has to purify the temple from the corruption and the profanation and the sin that have come into the cosmos."],[2732.21,"So he's talking about Christ's sacrifice."],[2734.45,"Now he notice he says the blood of his cross."],[2737.41,"So this isn't just a cosmic mythological thing."],[2744.21,"He is the lamb slain before the foundation of the world."],[2746.85,"But this isn't just this cosmic mythological thing."],[2749.81,"This cosmic reality corresponds to Christ incarnate bleeding, nailed to a wooden cross outside Jerusalem on a particular day at a particular time."],[2765.01,"That is for St."],[2765.97,"Paul, when it happened."],[2769.65,"But what he's just described to us, this cosmic thing, is what happened then."],[2776.85,"Okay. In what way is, if any, is the cosmic separate from the historical event, or is it?"],[2791.09,"It isn't. It isn't."],[2792.69,"Okay. Right. It isn't."],[2794.77,"So it doesn't have a separate reality."],[2797.57,"Right. It wasn't true before that happened, but it was true after that happened."],[2803.01,"Except that that God isn't in time."],[2805.09,"Yeah. Sorry. But that's when it's experienced in our reality."],[2809.33,"Those two things are the same thing for him."],[2811.41,"One doesn't represent the other."],[2813.41,"Yeah. It's not an analogy or an allegory."],[2816.69,"Uh-huh. Right? This is what you see, this is what is happening."],[2821.41,"Sort of like in the the icon of the Dormition of the Theotokos."],[2825.41,"At the lower level, you have what you see, which is the apostles mourning her and her body, and then the upper half is what's happening, which is Christ bearing her soul and taking it to heaven surrounded by the angels taking her into his presence."],[2844.97,"Those are the same event in the same icon."],[2847.93,"Right. Yeah. So is there from ancient um sacrifices?"],[2853.53,"Was there a quality of um the quality of the sacrifice translate to different levels of purity of the blood?"],[2860.81,"Well, ancient pagan sacrifices, they did some totally different stuff with the blood."],[2865.21,"This is one of the points of discontinuity."],[2868.17,"Like they would drink it."],[2869.45,"Yeah. I remember reading something where they would specifically sacrifice a young, um non uh a young goat uh goat that didn't breed."],[2882.97,"You know, they'd sacrifice that in the old goat they'd send out to the woods so that um one of the demons."],[2888.97,"Well, no, it wasn't it wasn't an old goat and a young goat."],[2891.77,"It wasn't no, it wasn't like the old priest or the young priest and the exorcist."],[2895.45,"No, it's it's because they cast lots for which goat would be which."],[2901.05,"Oh, okay. So they both had to be young pure goats, because either one of them could have been the goat."],[2906.73,"There's one, there's a goat for Yahweh to goat for the Zazel."],[2909.29,"Yeah, yeah. Yeah."],[2910.33,"And so either one, depending on how the lot fell, could end up being old could be either one."],[2915.53,"So there was no difference in quality between an old goat or a young goat."],[2918.97,"No, they were both young goats."],[2920.33,"Oh, they were young."],[2921.05,"They both had to be young goats."],[2922.41,"They both had to be No, you didn't you didn't use this to get rid of your old goat you didn't want anymore."],[2928.49,"Right? No, no, no."],[2933.45,"But that we won't go down that whole rabbit trail right now."],[2936.17,"But but um, but yeah, so specifically that's what he's talking about with the blood."],[2942.29,"Right. So St."],[2944.77,"Paul has gone big here."],[2947.49,"Right. The cosmos is is a temple, the creation is a temple."],[2952.61,"Right. And Christ's blood has purified it so that the church can now serve in that temple as his as uh his priest."],[2962.69,"That that would probably be enough for the night."],[2965.41,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole council of God."],[2972.77,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2978.05,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":36.14,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where we are in Colossians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Col 1:15</b>; got ~<b>halfway through ch. 1</b> last time</li><li class=\"d0\">Written to <b>Colossae</b>: small town in the <b>Lycus Valley</b>, near <b>Laodicea</b> and Hierapolis (\"Heliopolis\")</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>circular quality</b> though to one church</li><li class=\"d0\">\"A lot of ink spilled\" on the <b>Colossian heresy</b> - dissertations must be written</li><li class=\"d1\">no reason to assume <b>one group / one teaching</b>; reconstructions can't fit everything Paul says</li><li class=\"d1\">better: various places the community might run into <b>error</b>, like <b>Corinth</b> - coming to Christian teaching with a <b>pagan mindset</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No need to reconstruct a whole belief system</li><li class=\"d0\">First half of ch. 1 = <b>greetings</b>; opening lays out <b>themes</b> he'll develop</li></ul>"},{"t":240.78,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 1:15 - Image of the invisible God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Just said: Christ delivered us from <b>powers of darkness</b>, into his kingdom, <b>redemption through his blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>who Christ is</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Col 1:15</b>: \"the <b>image of the invisible God</b>, the <b>firstborn over all creation</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Caused \"no end of consternation\" - <b>Arians'</b> favorite verse</li><li class=\"d1\">well translated: firstborn <b>over</b> all creation, not <b>of</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First part: <b>invisible God = the Father</b>; Christ is the <b>image</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not new theology: Paul isn't wondering \"I'm a monotheist, so how does that work?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">drawing on what <b>nearly every Jew in the 1st c.</b> believed</li></ul>"},{"t":374.74,"lv":3,"h":"Two powers in heaven","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Well known: <b>more than one person who is God</b>, more than one person who is <b>Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Called <b>two powers in heaven</b> / a <b>second power</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <b>exousia</b> = <b>freedom to act</b> (not *dynameis*, ability)</li><li class=\"d1\">Yahweh is free; <b>angelic powers</b> act at his behest, <b>no authority of their own</b></li><li class=\"d1\">second power = another person with that <b>freedom</b> → different relationship to Yahweh than angels</li><li class=\"d0\">Other terms: <b>greater Yahweh / lesser Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>second hypostasis</b> - language predates Trinitarian stuff</li></ul>"},{"t":505.38,"lv":3,"h":"The figure who appears; the God no one sees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second person = figure who <b>appears</b> all through the OT</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>angel of the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>word of the Lord</b> in personal terms: stands by <b>Samuel's bed</b>, \"pokes <b>Jeremiah</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">people <b>see Yahweh</b>: elders with Moses on <b>Sinai</b> - \"eat and drink\"; Yahweh with two angels <b>eats with Abraham and Sarah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Yet: <b>no one can see God and live</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Exodus</b> same chapter: Moses speaks to God <b>face to face</b> as to a friend → then asks to see glory: \"can't see my face and live\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Jews concluded: an <b>invisible God</b> nobody sees, and <b>Yahweh who appears and talks</b> - still <b>one God</b>, two persons</li><li class=\"d0\">NT: <b>John 1</b> - \"no one has seen God at any time, but the <b>unique God</b> in the bosom of the Father <b>has made him known</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">John not \"retconning\" the OT - that's who you've been seeing</li></ul>"},{"t":683.82,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;amp;A: modalism vs. two figures; Son of Man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Any ancient Hebrew literature on <b>modalism</b> vs. two divine figures?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that comes later\" - will get there</li><li class=\"d0\">Other big figure: <b>Son of Man in Daniel</b> - <b>Ancient of Days</b> and Son of Man same figure</li><li class=\"d1\">why Christ calls himself <b>Son of Man</b> before the high priest</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>invisible God</b> and <b>image</b> who appears and makes him known</li><li class=\"d0\">NT consistent: second person became <b>incarnate as Jesus Christ</b> (cf. <b>Phil 2</b> hymn)</li></ul>"},{"t":763.54,"lv":3,"h":"Rabbinic reaction: heresy and Rabbi Akiva","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Crisis in <b>rabbinic Judaism</b> after Christianity: <b>1 Enoch, 2 Enoch</b> etc. all speak of the second figure</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbis see Christianity as a result</li><li class=\"d0\">2nd c., after temple and <b>Bar Kokhba</b>: rabbis <b>repudiate those texts</b>, declare <b>two powers in heaven = heresy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Problem in the <b>Talmud</b>: famous rabbis like <b>Akiva</b> believed it - \"because everybody did\"</li><li class=\"d1\">want to keep Akiva, ditch the idea → <b>conflicting stories</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Solution 1: Akiva <b>corrected by an angel</b> - \"one for judgment and one for mercy\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>thrones in Daniel</b>: one God (basically <b>modalism</b>) as old man for mercy, young man for judgment and justice</li><li class=\"d1\">two thrones = two purposes, not two persons</li></ul>"},{"t":913.62,"lv":3,"h":"Hagigah: the archangel who sat down","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Solution 2: <b>Tractate Hagigah</b> - rabbi they weren't trying to salvage</li><li class=\"d1\">mystical vision, ascends to heaven, sees <b>someone sitting</b> beside God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sitting = mark of authority</b> in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">opposite of liturgy today: ancient teacher <b>sits</b>, audience <b>stands</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Explained: an <b>archangel</b> being \"naughty\" by sitting</li><li class=\"d1\">thrown out, cursed, <b>whipped by angels</b>; rabbi who believed in two powers - cursed</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I know it's a weird story\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Metatron</b> stuff in <b>3 Enoch</b>: later rabbinic way to salvage that literature <b>without</b> saying more than one person of the Godhead</li></ul>"},{"t":1039.74,"lv":3,"h":"What \"image of the invisible\" means","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">At first glance in English nonsense: \"I took a picture of something invisible\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Meaning: the person of the Godhead who <b>reveals the Father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"You don't see the Father, you see the Son\"; know Christ → know the Father</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John</b> read on <b>Holy Thursday</b>: \"show us the Father\" - \"if you've seen me, you have seen the Father\" - not same person, but the <b>image</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Image</b> language picked up by the fathers of <b>Nicaea and Constantinople I</b>: <b>Gregory, Basil</b> - from Paul here</li><li class=\"d0\">Image reveals God not only in the <b>incarnation</b> (fulfillment) - begins way back</li><li class=\"d1\">\"somebody was walking in the garden\" <b>Gen 3</b> - \"which means they had feet and legs\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1156.74,"lv":2,"h":"\"Firstborn over all creation\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Arians</b>: <b>prototokos</b> (cf. *Theotokos*) = <b>born first</b>, first thing created</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Over</b>\" correct: firstborn in the ancient Jewish world = <b>status</b>, not chronology</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>heir</b>: got <b>100%</b>, then distributed</li><li class=\"d1\">next <b>head of household</b> (Roman *paterfamilias*)</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospels: man asks Jesus to tell his brother to <b>divide the inheritance</b> - brother was firstborn, kept it all</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus: why worry? <b>Follow me</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 9</b>: <b>Jacob and Esau</b> - Esau first by seconds, Jacob has firstborn status; <b>Ishmael</b> first, <b>Isaac</b> firstborn status</li><li class=\"d0\">So <b>over all creation</b>: Christ's status over <b>everything</b>, not just humanity</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Matt 28</b>: all <b>authority</b> (*exousia*) in heaven and earth given to me</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>shares it with us</b>: <b>co-heirs</b>, sons of God; shares his <b>rule</b> and <b>priesthood</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1370.42,"lv":3,"h":"Two points; Q&amp;amp;A on firstborn and Gentiles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Two things in v. 15: <b>1)</b> the <b>visible / bodily Yahweh</b> of the OT, reveals invisible Father; <b>2)</b> <b>authority as firstborn</b> over all creation</li><li class=\"d1\">connected: Christ as the <b>Son of Man</b> who inherits all the nations</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Why \"over\" - already ancient understanding?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's English\"; Arians pushed it as proof text, \"twisting\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Colossae = <b>Gentiles</b>, small Jewish community</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul just says <b>Jesus is that person</b> from the OT you know</li><li class=\"d1\">for us must <b>unpack</b> - the theology was <b>buried</b> for a long time</li><li class=\"d1\">chronic Western problem: <b>local synagogue</b> = what Judaism always was, even projected to Israelites in <b>1000 BC</b> - \"no\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1524.38,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 1:16 - All things created by, through, for him","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Col 1:16</b>: \"by him all things were created... in heaven and on earth, <b>visible and invisible</b>, <b>thrones, dominions, principalities, powers</b>... through him and for him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"For\" = <b>because</b>: if doubts about <b>Arian</b> reading, \"this kind of messes it up\"</li><li class=\"d1\">firstborn status because <b>he created everything</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Thrones, dominions...: all the spirits, <b>pagan gods</b> you worshiped, angels, demons - <b>Christ created them</b> → \"he ain't one of them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul and <b>prepositions</b>: <b>by</b>, <b>through</b>, <b>for</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1598.42,"lv":3,"h":"By, through and for: the Trinity creating","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>By him</b>: not just the Father doing it with Christ watching → can't be first thing created</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Through him</b>: he and the Father <b>cooperating</b>, not instead</li><li class=\"d1\">not the Father <b>and not him</b>, not him and not the Father</li><li class=\"d1\">vs. <b>Platonism</b>: remote Most High + <b>demiurge</b> below creating - \"Paul's clarifying that's not what he's saying\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Both <b>actively involved</b> at once → later fathers: Trinity has <b>one energy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"They don't work separately, let alone against each other\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>For him</b>: <b>eschatological</b> - firstborn / inheritance status</li><li class=\"d1\">at <b>ascension and enthronement</b> (<b>Daniel</b>, end of Gospels, start of Acts) Christ <b>receives all creation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">creation = inheritance of the firstborn - \"everything. Except the Father\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1730.78,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;amp;A: is Christ ever called uncreated?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Is Christ ever described as <b>uncreated</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">implied: all created things made by, through, for him → <b>can't be created</b> unless he created himself, \"which he didn't\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul has the category <b>things created</b> and Jesus is <b>outside</b> of it</li><li class=\"d1\">will look for an explicit text; the term used later to explain</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: hard to distinguish <b>creation vs. procession</b>; <b>begotten</b> and <b>proceeding</b> in the <b>Nicene Creed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"all we can tell is that these are two different things\" - \"we repeat back what we were told\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1847.78,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 1:17 - Before all, and in him all things hold together","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Col 1:17</b>: \"He is <b>before</b> all things, and in him all things <b>consist</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>is</b> before all things, not <b>was</b>\": doesn't come into being before the rest; all comes into being, <b>he already is</b></li><li class=\"d0\">*Consist* = literally <b>hold together</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Probably OT way of speaking about creation: not a <b>one-time event</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we think <b>deistically</b>: \"winds up the clock\" - \"you used to have to wind clocks, guys\"</li><li class=\"d1\">OT: creation <b>ongoing, perpetual</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every child conceived is <b>created by God in the womb</b>; crops and plants</li><li class=\"d0\">A building block of the <b>divine energies</b>: God working in creation</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: Christ not only \"ye olden times\" - not just \"helping wind the clock\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ongoing sustaining</b> and unfolding of creative activity</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: sometimes called the <b>creation psalm</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2043.86,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 1:18 - Head of the body, the church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Body</b> is not just analogy: your <b>nexus of powers and potentialities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how you <b>act and express yourself</b> in the world</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Head of the body, the church</b>: more than authority - he <b>directs</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">church = means by which Christ <b>communicates and interacts with the world</b>: hands, feet, parts</li><li class=\"d1\">authority <b>in</b> the church, <b>acts through</b> the church, within creation</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's view of church and creation: <b>Christ creator and sustainer of the cosmos</b>; church the means \"in the midst\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He's the <b>beginning, firstborn from the dead</b> → \"in all things he has the <b>preeminence</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">full circle back to what <b>firstborn</b> means</li></ul>"},{"t":2137.66,"lv":2,"h":"Colossians 1:19-20 - All fullness; reconciling all things","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Col 1:19</b>: \"it <b>pleased</b> the Father that in him all the <b>fullness</b> should dwell, and by him to <b>reconcile all things</b> to himself... <b>having made peace through the blood of his cross</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pleased</b> (King James, Elizabethan) = <b>will or desire</b>, not \"made happy\"</li><li class=\"d1\">troparia of the resurrection: Christ \"<b>took pleasure</b> in ascending the cross\" - \"crucifixion, not a fun time\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>willingly, voluntarily</b>; \"wasn't nailed... and pushed up in the air\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same here: the <b>Father's will</b> to reconcile the whole creation through Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Two categories: first, creation by, through, for Christ - <b>no sin, no need for reconciliation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">second: <b>sin enters</b>; Father wills Christ also <b>take care of the sin issue</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all creation <b>affected by sin</b> → <b>alienated from God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in addition to incarnation, ascension, inheritance: <b>reconcile creation through his blood</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2309.02,"lv":3,"h":"All things: the whole creation, not just humans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"reconciling all things... whether things on earth or in heaven\" - <b>not humans only</b></li><li class=\"d1\">includes humans but also <b>rocks, trees, fish, fowl</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peace through the blood of the cross happens to the <b>whole creation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the problem: sin <b>tainted the whole creation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">must be <b>cleansed and purified</b> to come back into God's presence</li><li class=\"d1\">sinful thing in his presence → <b>consumed by his holiness</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2380.86,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;amp;A: sin's effect on non-human creation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: How do we see the impact of sin on non-human creation?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't recommend playing with an <b>alligator</b>\" - <b>enmity</b> between humans and animals, and each other</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Adam and the ground</b>: thorns and thistles; natural disasters</li><li class=\"d0\">Whatever your politics on <b>climate change</b>: sinful human exploitation harming the world is \"<b>basic to Christianity</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"pollute your river and then try and drink out of it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sin affects people <b>physically</b>: <b>corruption and death</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Saints with wild animals - <b>Seraphim and the bear</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Mark</b>: after temptation, Christ \"among the <b>wild animals</b>\" 40 days</li></ul>"},{"t":2553.57,"lv":2,"h":"Blood purifies the cosmic temple","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All creation purified - esp. <b>Day of Atonement</b></li><li class=\"d1\">blood not \"to feed the <b>blood god</b>\" - used to <b>purify</b> the tabernacle's physical objects, <b>rededicate</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sinai covenant</b>: blood <b>sprinkled on the people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we see blood and think the opposite - \"how are you gonna get that blood stain out\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why: sin = <b>taint and power of death</b>; <b>blood is life</b> → \"smear life on it to overcome death\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same language for <b>Christ's blood</b>: <b>Hebrews, 1 John</b> - purifies, cleanses from sin</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul at the <b>cosmic level</b>: treats whole cosmos</li><li class=\"d1\">back to <b>Genesis 1</b>: structured like ancient <b>temple-building narratives</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eden = temple</b>; <b>Adam</b> created to serve as <b>priest</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for that destiny: Christ's blood must purify the temple from <b>corruption, profanation, sin</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2732.21,"lv":2,"h":"The blood of his cross: cosmic and historical","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not just cosmic myth: \"<b>blood of his cross</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lamb slain before the foundation of the world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">corresponds to Christ incarnate <b>bleeding on a wooden cross</b> outside Jerusalem, <b>a particular day, a particular time</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Is cosmic separate from the historical event?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"It isn't\" - <b>no separate reality</b>; God not in time</li><li class=\"d1\">one doesn't <b>represent</b> the other - not <b>analogy or allegory</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Dormition icon</b>: lower half what you see (apostles, body); upper half what's happening (<b>Christ bearing her soul</b>) - same event</li></ul>"},{"t":2847.93,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;amp;A: sacrifice quality; the two goats","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Did quality of sacrifice change purity of blood?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pagan sacrifices</b> did different things with blood - a point of <b>discontinuity</b>; \"they would <b>drink</b> it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Day of Atonement <b>goats</b>: <b>lots cast</b>, one for <b>Yahweh</b>, one for <b>Azazel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">both had to be <b>young, pure</b> - could end up either one</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you didn't use this to get rid of your old goat\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Won't go down that rabbit trail</li></ul>"},{"t":2936.17,"lv":2,"h":"Closing: the cosmos as temple","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"St. Paul has gone big here\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cosmos / creation is a temple</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ's blood has purified it</b> so the <b>church</b> can serve in it as his <b>priest</b></li></ul>"}]});
